.md
## The growth function

The potential `U` is not yet an update. Lenia passes it through a non-monotone growth curve:

$$
G_{\\mu,\\sigma}(u)
=2\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{(u-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right]-1.
$$

A neighborhood close to the preferred value $\\mu$ grows. Values farther away decay, while $\\sigma$ controls the width of the favorable band. This maps the potential to a signed rate between approximately $-1$ and $1$.

This is also where the neural-network analogy becomes less literal. Unlike a monotone activation such as ReLU, the Lenia response selects a narrow interval of neighborhood densities.

The preview below uses $\\mu=2\\times0.16=0.32$ and $\\sigma=0.021$. `ImageAdjust` changes only the displayed contrast only to see the effect better

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## The growth function

The potential `U` is not yet an update. Lenia passes it through a non-monotone growth curve:

$$
G_{\\mu,\\sigma}(u)
=2\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{(u-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right]-1.
$$

A neighborhood close to the preferred value $\\mu$ grows. Values farther away decay, while $\\sigma$ controls the width of the favorable band. This maps the potential to a signed rate between approximately $-1$ and $1$.

This is also where the neural-network analogy becomes less literal. Unlike a monotone activation such as ReLU, the Lenia response selects a narrow interval of neighborhood densities.

The preview below uses $\\mu=2\\times0.16=0.32$ and $\\sigma=0.021$. `ImageAdjust` changes only the displayed contrast only to see the effect better

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
G[\[Mu]_, \[Sigma]_][U_] := ImageApply[Function[a, 2 Exp[- (*FB[*)(((*SpB[*)Power[(a - \[Mu])(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*))(*,*)/(*,*)(2 (*SpB[*)Power[\[Sigma](*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*)))(*]FB*)]-1.0], U]

G[2 0.16, 0.021][U[20, 4, {1.0, 2/3, 1/3}][canvas]] // ImageAdjust

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["6a41d2f0-4c6f-4e1e-8e6f-5bb0edd25597"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKmyWaGKYYpRnomiSbpemapBqm6lqkAlmmSUkGqSkpRqamluYAi3QWCA=="*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Close the loop

Now we can put convolution and growth together:

$$
A^{t+\\Delta t}
=\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}
\\left(A^t+\\Delta t\\,G_{\\mu,\\sigma}(K*A^t)\\right).
$$

The code below stores the convolution operator in `potential`, keeps the current image in `field`, and advances it with $\\Delta t=0.1$. `Clip` is part of the rule: it keeps every state inside $[0,1]$.

There are two clocks in this cell. The model advances by $0.1$ per update, while `Refresh[..., 1/60.0]` asks the notebook to redraw at roughly 60 Hz. 

With only these few lines, the image becomes a recurrent dynamical system.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Close the loop

Now we can put convolution and growth together:

$$
A^{t+\\Delta t}
=\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}
\\left(A^t+\\Delta t\\,G_{\\mu,\\sigma}(K*A^t)\\right).
$$

The code below stores the convolution operator in `potential`, keeps the current image in `field`, and advances it with $\\Delta t=0.1$. `Clip` is part of the rule: it keeps every state inside $[0,1]$.

There are two clocks in this cell. The model advances by $0.1$ per update, while `Refresh[..., 1/60.0]` asks the notebook to redraw at roughly 60 Hz. 

With only these few lines, the image becomes a recurrent dynamical system.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
potential = U[30, 4,  {1.0, 2/3, 1/3}];
field = canvas;

Refresh[Colorize[
  field = Clip[
    field + 0.1 G[0.16, 0.011][potential[field]], 
  {0.,1.}]], 
1/60.0]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Record a trajectory

A live `Refresh` loop is useful while experimenting, but a fixed animation is easier to inspect and share. Here we compute 60 updates first and then pass the frames to `AnimatedImage`.

Since $\\Delta t=0.1$, the trajectory covers 6 model-time units. Playing 60 frames at 30 frames per second produces a two-second animation. These are separate quantities: changing `FrameRate` changes the movie, not the Lenia dynamics.

The assignment `field = canvas` makes the cell reproducible. Every evaluation starts from the same image.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Record a trajectory

A live `Refresh` loop is useful while experimenting, but a fixed animation is easier to inspect and share. Here we compute 60 updates first and then pass the frames to `AnimatedImage`.

Since $\\Delta t=0.1$, the trajectory covers 6 model-time units. Playing 60 frames at 30 frames per second produces a two-second animation. These are separate quantities: changing `FrameRate` changes the movie, not the Lenia dynamics.

The assignment `field = canvas` makes the cell reproducible. Every evaluation starts from the same image.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
potential = U[30, 4,  {1.0, 2/3, 1/3}];
field = canvas;

AnimatedImage[Colorize/@Table[
    field = Clip[
    field + 0.1 G[0.16, 0.011][potential[field]], 
  {0.,1.}], {60}], FrameRate->30]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
